\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Now Yahweh¦370574 told Yonah¦370577 (\add Jonah,\add* Amittai's¦370580 son),\x + \xo 1:1: \xt 2Ki 14:25.\x*
\v 2 “Go¦370584 now to¦370585 the major city¦370588 of Nineveh¦370587, and¦370590 speak out against it¦370594 because¦370592 I've noticed how evil they are.”
\p
\v 3 But¦370598 Yonah¦370599 decided to run away from Yahweh so he¦370606 went down to Yafo¦370605 \add !(Joppa¦370605)\add* and found¦370606 a ship¦370607 about¦370610 to go to Tarshish. He¦370612 paid the fare¦370613 and went aboard to go with¦370615 them¦370617 to Tarshish to get away from Yahweh.
\p
\v 4 However Yahweh¦370622 caused a strong wind¦370624 on¦370634 the sea¦370629 and¦370622 it¦370630 became a such big storm¦370631 that they all thought¦370636 the ship¦370635 would disintegrate.
\v 5 The sailors¦370640 were afraid¦370639 and they each¦370642 cried¦370641 to their own gods for help¦370641, and they threw¦370646 the cargo overboard to make the ship¦370651 lighter¦370655. But¦370639 Yonah¦370657 had gone¦370658 down into¦370659 the hold where he¦370663 had laid down and fallen¦370664 fast asleep¦370664.
\p
\v 6 So¦370666 the ship's captain approached¦370666 him and said¦370670, “Why are you¦370674 sleeping¦370675? Come on, pray to your¦370680 god¦370680 and perhaps¦370681 he¦370682 will¦370682 take notice of us¦370684 so we¦370686 won't¦370685 die.”
\p
\v 7 Then¦370688 the sailors said¦370688 to each other, “Let's draw straws to find out whose¦370697 fault it¦370703 is that¦370697 we¦370696 are in¦370697 this predicament.” So¦370688 they¦370688 drew straws and¦370688 Yonah¦370707 was indicated.
\v 8 So¦370709 they¦370709 said¦370709 to him¦370710, “Hey, tell¦370711 us why this¦370719 disaster is happening to us. What's¦370721 your¦370723 occupation¦370723? Where¦370724 are you from? What country¦370727? What nationality are you?”
\p
\v 9 And he¦370734 said¦370734 to¦370735 them¦370735, “I'm¦370743 a Hebrew¦370736 and I fear¦370744 Yahweh¦370740 the god¦370741 of¦370741 the heavens¦370742 who¦370745 made¦370747 the sea¦370750 and the land.”
\p
\v 10 And¦370759 the¦370756 men¦370756 became really scared¦370755 and¦370755 asked him¦370760 what¦370761 he had done¦370764, because¦370765 he had told¦370776 them¦370777 that¦370763 he was running from Yahweh¦370772.
\p
\v 11 So¦370788 they asked him what¦370781 they should do¦370783 to¦370784 him so¦370785 that¦370785 the sea¦370786 would calm, because¦370788 the storm¦370791 was¦370790 getting worse.
\p
\v 12 And he¦370793 said¦370793 to¦370794 them¦370794, “Pick¦370795 me up¦370795 and¦370793 throw¦370796 me into¦370797 the sea so that the sea will calm down because I¦370805 know¦370804 that this terrible storm¦370808 came because of¦370807 me.”
\p
\v 13 However¦370821, the men¦370814 tried to row but¦370813 they¦370820 were not able¦370820 to return to the land because¦370821 the sea¦370822 was¦370823 very rough and¦370819 getting worse.
\v 14 So¦370827 they¦370827 called¦370827 to¦370828 Yahweh and said¦370831, “We beg you Yahweh: please¦370852 don't let¦370837 us die because of¦370838 this man¦370839, and don't consider us guilty because you yourself have¦370853 made this happen.”
\v 15 And then¦370855 they¦370855 picked¦370855 up¦370855 Yonah¦370858 and threw¦370859 him¦370859 into¦370860 the sea, and the sea became calm.
\v 16 The¦370877 men¦370868 felt really scared¦370867 of Yahweh¦370873 and they¦370867 sacrificed¦370876 to¦370877 him and made vows¦370879.
\rem We renumbered this chapter to match the LV for now
\c 2
\rem /s1 Jonah's Prayer; A Psalm of Thanksgiving
\p
\v 1 Then¦370891 Yahweh¦370883 commanded a huge¦370885 fish to¦370886 swallow¦370886 Yonah¦370889 and he¦370890 stayed in¦370892 the belly¦370892 of¦370892 the fish for three days¦370895 and three nights¦370897.\x + \xo 1:17: \xt Mat 12:40.\x*
\s1 Yonah's prayer
\p
\v 2 And¦370900 Yonah¦370901 prayed¦370900 to¦370902 Yahweh¦370904 his¦370905 god¦370905 from¦370906 the belly¦370906 of¦370906 the fish¦370907.
\v 3 And¦370910 he¦370910 said¦370910,
\q1 “I called¦370911 to you Yahweh¦370916 in my distress¦370912 and you answered¦370917 me¦370917. I cried¦370920 for¦370920 help¦370920 from¦370918 the¦370918 valley of¦370918 death and you heard¦370921 my voice¦370922.
\v 4 You¦370925 have¦370925 thrown¦370925 me right into the deep—the heart¦370927 of¦370927 the sea; and¦370929 a flood surrounds¦370930 me. All¦370931 your¦370933 waves¦370934 are crashing over¦370935 me.
\q1
\v 5 And¦370939 I said¦370940, ‘I have¦370941 been¦370941 driven¦370941 out of¦370943 your sight¦370943 however I will¦370945 look¦370946 again towards your holy temple¦370949.’
\q1
\v 6 I was up to¦370955 my neck¦370957 in the waters¦370954; it¦370959 was deep¦370958 all around me¦370959. There were reeds wrapped¦370961 around my head¦370962.
\q1
\v 7 I¦370967 went¦370967 down to the foundations of the mountains¦370966; barred from returning to the land¦370968. But¦370972 you¦370972 saved my life¦370974 from the grave, Yahweh¦370975 my god¦370976.
\q1
\v 8 When¦370979 my life¦370981 was draining away¦370979 from me¦370980, I¦370985 remembered¦370985 you, Yahweh¦370984, and¦370986 my prayer¦370988 reached you in¦370979 your holy temple¦370991.
\q1
\v 9 Those¦370995 who¦370995 worship lifeless gods abandon¦371000 their¦370999 allegiance to you.
\q1
\v 10 But¦371003 I'll speak out my gratitude as I sacrifice¦371006 to you¦371008. I've¦371010 vowed¦371010 this and¦371003 I'll do it, because Yahweh¦371013 is my rescuer¦371012.”
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh¦371018 spoke¦371017 to¦371019 the fish¦371019 and¦371017 it¦371020 vomited¦371020 Yonah¦371023 up¦371020 onto the land.
\c 3
\s1 Yonah goes to Nineveh
\rem /s1 Jonah Obeys the Lord; Conversion of Nineveh; Jonah Goes to Nineveh
\p
\v 1 And¦371029 Yahweh¦371032 spoke to Yonah¦371035 a second¦371036 time saying¦371037,
\v 2 “Go¦371040 now to the major city¦371044, Nineveh¦371043, and¦371046 announce this declaration which¦371052 I've¦371053 told you¦371055.”
\p
\v 3 So¦371057 Yonah¦371058 went¦371059 directly to Nineveh¦371062. It¦371067 was¦371067 a¦371068 large¦371070 city¦371068, taking three¦371073 days¦371074 to travel across.
\v 4 Yonah¦371077 went one¦371082 day's¦371081 travel into the city¦371079 and¦371083 publicly announced, “Nineveh¦371088 is¦371089 going to¦371089 be¦371089 destroyed in¦371079 forty¦371086 days¦371087!”\x + \xo 3:4-5: \xt Mat 12:41; Luk 11:32.\x*
\p
\v 5 The¦371092 people of Nineveh¦371093 believed¦371091 in¦371094 God¦371094 and announced a time of fasting, and from the least¦371103 to the most important people, they just wore¦371098 sackcloth¦371099 \add as a sign of contrition\add*.
\p
\v 6 The king¦371109 of Nineveh¦371111 also heard about¦371109 it and he¦371112 got down from¦371113 his throne¦371113, took¦371114 off¦371114 his robe¦371115 and put on¦371120 sackcloth¦371118, then¦371105 sat¦371119 in a pile of ashes¦371122.
\v 7 And he had¦371124 a statement written out and publicly read throughout Nineveh¦371126, saying¦371125, “The king¦371128 and his advisors declare that people and livestock¦371132, be it herds or flocks¦371134, are prohibited from grazing or eating anything¦371138, and even from drinking¦371145 water¦371142.
\v 8 Both people and livestock¦371150 should just wear sacks, and they should use their¦371164 strength to cry out¦371151 to God¦371154 and they should turn¦371156 away from¦371158 the¦371159 evil¦371159 and violent¦371162 things they have been doing.
\v 9 And maybe God¦371171 will¦371169 turn¦371169 and change his mind and stop his fierce¦371173 anger¦371174, and maybe we¦371176 won't¦371175 die.”
\p
\v 10 And¦371180 God¦371179 saw¦371178 their actions in turning¦371185 away from¦371186 their evil¦371187 ways¦371186 and he changed his mind about¦371182 the destruction which¦371193 he had¦371195 said¦371195 he would send to¦371198 them¦371198 and he didn't¦371199 proceed with it.
\c 4
\s1 Yonah gets upset
\rem /s1 Jonah's Anger; Jonah's Anger and God's Mercy; Jonah's Anger at the Lord's Compassion; Jonah's Anger at the Lord's Mercy
\p
\v 1 But¦371202 Yonah¦371205 was¦371202 very upset and¦371208 the situation made him¦371209 angry.
\v 2 And¦371215 he¦371215 said to Yahweh, “Please¦371216 Yahweh, wasn't this¦371220 what I said would happen when I was¦371224 still in my¦371227 own¦371227 country¦371227? That's why I fled¦371232 to Tarshish¦371233 the first¦371231 time¦371231, because¦371234 I knew¦371235 that¦371220 you¦371237 are¦371238 a caring forgiving god¦371238, who doesn't get angry quickly; full of kindness and¦371211 not wanting to cause distress.\x + \xo 4:2: \xt Exo 34:6.\x*
\v 3 So¦371252 therefore Yahweh¦371253, please¦371256 kill me¦371260 because¦371261 I'd¦371257 rather die¦371263 than¦371264 live¦371264 like this.”\x + \xo 4:3: \xt 1Ki 19:4.\x*
\p
\v 4 But¦371267 Yahweh¦371268 said¦371267, “Do¦371269 you think it's¦371270 right¦371269 to¦371271 be angry like that?”
\p
\v 5 And Yonah¦371274 went¦371273 outside the city to the east¦371279 and made¦371281 himself a shelter¦371284 and sat¦371285 there¦371283 in¦371287 the shade¦371287 to see¦371290 what¦371291 would happen to the city.
\v 6 And Yahweh¦371297 God¦371299 commanded a plant and it¦371301 grew up over Yonah to shade¦371306 his head¦371309 and to relieve him¦371311 from¦371312 his distress¦371312. Yonah was very pleased about¦371312 the¦371317 plant.
\v 7 Then¦371321 at¦371324 dawn¦371325 the next day¦371326 God¦371328 commanded a grub and¦371321 it attacked¦371327 the plant¦371330 which then¦371321 shrivelled up.
\v 8 And when the sun came up, God¦371338 sent a hot¦371341 east¦371340 wind¦371340 and the sun beat on¦371344 Yonah's¦371347 head¦371346. He¦371348 became¦371348 faint¦371348 and asked¦371349 for his life to end and said¦371354, “It's¦371354 better for me to die¦371353 than¦371357 to live.”
\rem /s1 Jonah Is Reproved
\p
\v 9 And¦371371 God¦371360 said¦371359 to Yonah¦371363, “Do¦371364 you think it's¦371365 right¦371372 to be angry about the¦371370 plant¦371370?”
\p And Yonah replied¦371359, “Yes, I'm angry enough to¦371361 die!”
\p
\v 10 Then¦371380 Yahweh¦371381 said¦371380, “You felt sorry for the¦371386 plant which¦371387 you didn't¦371388 even plant or work for. It came up in a day and¦371380 died again in a day
\v 11 so shouldn't¦371404 I¦371405 feel sorry for the huge¦371410 city¦371409 of Nineveh¦371408 which¦371411 has¦371405 so many¦371415 people in¦371414 it¦371414 who¦371421 don't¦371404 know right¦371427 from wrong—over 120,000 people, not¦371422 to mention all the livestock¦371429!”
